ker on a low, rainbow board (2♦, 5♥, 4♣). The pre-flop action was passive (Check-Check), suggesting neither player has a premium starting hand. My hand has showdown value, but it is vulnerable to overcards on the turn and river. The straight draw (any 3) is also a concern. Betting might fold out random overcards, but it also risks building a pot with a marginal hand that cannot withstand significant pressure. Checking is the safest option to control the pot size and realize equity.\n\nReason:\nWith bottom pair in a limped pot, the objective is pot control. The hand is not strong enough to value bet for three streets, and betting now might isolate me against better pairs or strong draws. Checking leaves the opportunity to bluff-catch or improve on later streets without committing more chips unnecessarily.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
